{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788168077,"nonce":"Qffcy93yGTxObYr/++KJ22E1pPA=","message":"Fixed in the same branch: merged into ONE job, `ci/test-and-coverage`. The\n`test` job is deleted (68 lines) and `.github/required-checks.txt` carries a\nsingle `required-if-run ci/test-and-coverage` line.\n\nThe trade in my earlier comment turned out not to be a trade. The case for\nkeeping a separate `test` was insurance: a cargo-llvm-cov or\n`llvm-tools-preview` breakage would at least leave a test signal. But\n`ci/coverage` was ALREADY required, so such a breakage already blocked every\nmerge whether or not `test` sat beside it green. The suite was being run twice\nto buy a hedge that did not exist. `cargo llvm-cov nextest` exits non-zero on a\nred suite, so the merged job reports a strict superset of what `test` reported.\n\nTwo things I measured rather than assumed, because they decide whether the\nalternative (keep both, let `coverage` gate only the ratchet) was even\nbuildable:\n\n  plain `cargo llvm-cov nextest` + 1 red test  -\u003e  exit 100, NO lcov written\n  same + `--ignore-run-fail`                   -\u003e  exit 0, full lcov,\n                                                   LF:83 LH:75 == the green run\n\nBoth matter. The first means the ratchet step cannot even run on a red suite\nwithout the flag. The second means the flag would make this job GO GREEN WITH\nFAILING TESTS — so it must never be added, and the workflow comment says so at\nthe point of temptation. It also refutes my earlier guess that a red run would\ndistort the coverage numbers: `--ignore-run-fail` implies `--no-fail-fast`, so\nevery test still executes and still covers its lines.\n\n`just test` keeps its plain un-instrumented `cargo nextest`. The local inner\nloop should not pay for instrumentation; this was about CI paying twice.","files":null}]}